          { name: "Aging Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 50.4 median age with 76.8% homeownership and $54,946 income, MT-HD-14 is a district of older homeowners whose fixed incomes are under pressure from rising property taxes, healthcare costs, and rural service decline. These households own assets but have constrained monthly budgets — a demographic segment that values Republican fiscal positioning but will punish representatives who fail to deliver on cost containment for specific necessities like healthcare and property taxes. Material governance competence on these issues is the foundation of holding this district's 75% margin." },
          { name: "Rural Service Viability & Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 24.1% college-attainment rate and lower-middle median income in rural Montana signal a constituency dependent on public services — rural healthcare delivery, road maintenance, water infrastructure — that are becoming harder to fund and sustain. The aging population composition amplifies rural healthcare accessibility as a direct constituent concern; older adults cannot commute long distances to specialists or emergency care. Demonstrating specific legislative action or budget priority on rural service delivery is essential to consolidating voter confidence in a district where service decline is an observable, present problem." },
          { name: "Native American Population & Governance", tag: "careful", why: "The 8.5% demographic gap in the Census data is unaccounted for and in rural Montana likely represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents — a population that may comprise 8-10% of the district. This could include tribal nations with specific governance priorities (tribal sovereignty, IHS healthcare access, land management), and current representation may not be engaging these communities as governing partners. A challenger who activates this constituency around specific tribal concerns could shift a meaningful percentage of votes; the current 75% margin assumes AIAN constituents are not mobilized around distinct governance priorities." },
          { name: "Treating 75% as an Immutable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% result in a district with 50.4 median age, $54,946 income, and an 8.5% unaccounted-for population is not a supermajority immune to challenger pressure — it is a substantial but contingent margin that rests on the incumbent successfully addressing older homeowners' cost-of-living concerns and not actively alienating a Native American constituency that may have distinct governance interests. Any candidate who campaigns as though the 25% opposition is demographically fixed or geographically concentrated enough to ignore will fail to identify emerging vulnerability." },
          { name: "Assuming Rural Economic Decline Is Inevitable", tag: "avoid", why: "While rural service decline and population aging are observable trends in Montana, a representative who defaults to accepting these as inevitable rather than proposing specific legislative or budget responses to reverse them will lose credibility with an older, fixed-income constituency for whom these trends translate directly to personal economic insecurity. This district's voters expect problem-solving on rural viability, not resignation to demographic fate." }
