        memoHeadline: "MT-HD-16: A 59-41 Democratic Win in a Working-Class, Renter-Heavy District with a Large Native American Population and College-Education Gap to Close.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MT-HD-16 is a working-class district defined by economic constraint and demographic complexity. At $45,156 median household income — the lowest threshold for material financial pressure — and a 32.5% renter rate, this is a constituency of renters and modest-income homeowners for whom housing costs, healthcare access, and wage stability are direct survival concerns. The Democratic 59-41 victory in 2024 reflects coalition alignment around economic security and social investment, but the 41% Republican share signals a meaningful constituency that either rejected the Democratic message or has not yet been persuaded that Democratic governance improves their material situation. At 33.7 median age and 20.2% college attainment, this is a younger, less-educated district than the statewide average — which means that economic mobility pathways, vocational training accessibility, and the cost of education are material policy concerns, not abstract talking points. The demographic gap (White 22.72%, Hispanic 1.63%, Asian 0.76%, Black 0.24% = 25.35% accounted for) leaves approximately 74.65% unaccounted for — a gap almost certainly dominated by Native American / Alaska Native population, making this a majority-AIAN district in demographic terms despite the Democratic partisan result.",
          "The 59-41 Democratic margin is not as durable as it appears if the underlying constituency is not actively served. A 41% Republican share in a district this economically stressed suggests that the opposition message — lower taxes, less regulation, or skepticism of government — maintains salience despite demonstrated economic hardship. This indicates either that Democratic economic messaging is not landing with sufficient specificity, or that the Democratic incumbent is not yet delivering visible material improvement in housing costs, healthcare access, or wage opportunity for the 41% who voted against the incumbent in 2024. The majority-AIAN population (approximately 75% of the district) creates a governance obligation: any representative in MT-HD-16 must engage tribal governments and Native American constituents as institutional partners around healthcare access (Indian Health Service coordination), economic development, and the state-tribal relationship. A Democratic incumbent in this district holds a narrow 18-point majority in a state where Republicans are dominant — meaning the coalition must be actively renewed, opposition concerns must be specifically addressed, and the Native American majority cannot be assumed to vote Democratic out of habit or loyalty."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with material economic security: housing affordability, healthcare cost management, and wage-supporting job access are the survival-level concerns for a $45,156-income, 32.5% renter constituency — not abstract progressive values. Demonstrate concrete policy wins or legislative momentum on these fronts.",
          "Build authentic partnership with tribal governments and Native American constituents as the demographic majority (~75% of the district): IHS healthcare coordination, tribal economic development, and state-tribal water/resource management are the specific governance issues that affect most people who live in MT-HD-16.",
          "Address the college-education gap (20.2%, lowest in most comparative contexts) with specific vocational and technical training pathways: for a younger (33.7 median age) working-class district, accessible post-secondary education is an economic mobility mechanism, not a luxury.",
          "Take the 41% opposition seriously as a constituency with legitimate economic concerns that deserve specific response: if Republican messaging on lower costs and less regulation resonates with nearly half the district, the Democratic incumbent must explain concretely why Democratic governance will deliver material improvement better than the alternative."
        ]
