          { name: "Treating Renter Households as a Secondary or Invisible Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 31.3%, renters represent nearly 1 in 3 households in MT-HD-28, yet renter-specific economic concerns (housing costs relative to wages, lease stability, community investment, access to credit for asset-building) are often invisible in homeowner-focused legislative messaging and governance. Renter households also turn over more frequently than homeowners, making them electorally more volatile; a legislator who speaks to renters' economic reality and demonstrates concrete delivery on housing affordability and community stability can capture persuadable votes, while one who defaults to homeowner-dominant framing cedes this constituency to opposition messaging. In a district with this renter percentage and this narrow Democratic opposition, neglecting renter economic concerns is electorally indefensible." }
