          "The 16.9% college attainment rate is the single lowest demographic indicator in the district and reveals a constituency whose economic pathway is not through credential-dependent professions but through wages, small business, and trade work. At this income and education level, candidates are not winning on abstract ideology — they are winning or losing on whether voters believe government is addressing their concrete daily constraints: childcare costs, healthcare access, housing affordability, and wage growth. Smith's 60% reflects consolidation of these voters around a perception of Democratic concern for working people, but that consolidation is contingent. In a district where the median household is earning $48,300 and one-third of households are renting, a cost-of-living crisis is not a policy debate — it is an active household budget crisis. Any challenger who can tie the incumbent to policies or fiscal decisions that have demonstrably worsened affordability for working families will find receptive ears among the 40% who did not vote for Smith, and potentially among swing voters in the 60% coalition who are evaluating whether their economic situation is improving or deteriorating under current representation."
