          { name: "AIAN Community Engagement & Tribal Partnership", tag: "lean-into", why: "The eleven-percentage-point demographic gap (~11-12% of the district population) represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents whose tribal governments, healthcare needs, water rights, and economic development are substantive governance concerns requiring active partnership. Native American communities in Montana have demonstrated capacity to organize around specific issues and to hold representatives accountable for engagement quality. Building genuine institutional relationships with AIAN constituents — rather than token acknowledgment — prevents the emergence of organized opposition from this constituency and reflects the material diversity of the district's population." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency Representation & Stability", tag: "careful", why: "At 43.6% of the district, renters form a substantial minority whose economic interests (housing costs, tenant protections, wage growth) are materially different from homeowners' interests (property values, tax policy). Renters as a constituency tend to be younger, more mobile, and more responsive to perceived economic opportunity and perceived neglect. In a 66-34 district, a shift in renter engagement or turnout — or a credible opposition candidate who speaks directly to renter economic concerns — is mathematically capable of narrowing the margin. Attending to this constituency with specificity, rather than as an afterthought to homeowner-focused messaging, is necessary to maintain the current Republican alignment." },
          { name: "Treating 66-34 as a Permanent Safe Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 32-point margin is substantial and provides real protection against ordinary electoral headwinds, but it is not a guarantee of durability in a district with below-average college attainment, below-median income, and 43.6% renters facing housing pressure. In districts with economically constrained younger populations, margins can erode quickly when economic conditions deteriorate, when opposition organizes around specific constituency concerns, or when the incumbent is perceived as inattentive to material quality-of-life issues. Any strategy that relies on the 66-34 result as a permanent floor rather than as a current market position vulnerable to organized challenge is strategically dangerous." },
          { name: "Symbolic AIAN Engagement Without Institutional Partnership", tag: "avoid", why: "The AIAN constituency at ~11-12% of the district cannot be governed around or satisfied with ceremonial acknowledgment of tribal culture without substantive engagement on healthcare access, water rights, economic development, and tribal sovereignty. Any representative whose engagement with AIAN constituents is reduced to symbolic events or surface-level acknowledgments without genuine institutional partnership with tribal governments will face organized opposition from this constituency. In a 66-34 district, losing AIAN voter enthusiasm or turnout can meaningfully narrow the margin and create electoral vulnerability." }
