          { name: "Education Access and Higher Education Workforce Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 29% college-educated adults in a district with a 32.3-year median age, MT-HD-51 has a younger, educationally diverse constituency where higher education access, student debt, and the pathway from postsecondary education to local employment are concrete constituent concerns. The 42.9% renter rate and moderate household income suggest many of these households have education debt or are evaluating the cost-benefit of higher education for their children. Whether this district is near a university, community college, or is experiencing outmigration of young talent, education access and affordability are the issues most likely to unlock engagement from a younger, less college-saturated electorate." },
          { name: "Young Adult Turnout and Opposition Mobilization", tag: "careful", why: "A 57-43 Republican margin in a district with a 32.3-year median age — substantially younger than statewide Montana — suggests that opposition turnout may be suppressed rather than support being genuinely robust. Young adults, renters, and less college-educated voters have historically lower turnout rates, and a 57% result could represent a plurality of a contested electorate rather than a stable majority. Any incumbent should treat the 43% opposition not as a fixed floor but as a latent mobilizable constituency; a credible Democratic challenger focused on housing, healthcare, and economic opportunity could shift this margin materially." },
          { name: "Treating Housing and Renter Concerns as Marginal Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 42.9% of households are renters at $64,826 median income, housing policy is not a boutique issue — it is a foundational economic security concern for nearly half the constituency. Any candidate whose messaging defaults to abstract tax or regulation positions without specific engagement on housing affordability, tenant rights, or the relationship between wages and rent will be vulnerable to an opponent who speaks directly to these households' material constraints." },
          { name: "Assuming Inherited Republican Loyalty Among Younger Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "At 32.3 median age, MT-HD-51 has the youngest constituency among comparable districts, and younger voters — particularly renters and those without college education — do not inherit partisan loyalty from their parents' generation. A 57% result is not a baseline for future elections; it is a current position that must be actively maintained through engagement on the issues that matter to people in their 20s, 30s, and early 40s. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this district as though Republican support is automatic will find that assumption tested by mobilized opposition." }
