          { name: "Education Funding & K-12 Accountability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 52.8% college attainment and 44.0 median age, MT-HD-53 has a high proportion of educated parents with children in or approaching school age; these voters have strong preferences about education spending, school quality, and accountability. State education funding levels, school choice, teacher compensation, and curriculum transparency are the policy levers that directly affect these families' quality of life and their children's outcomes. A representative who engages with specificity on education policy rather than generic support for 'schools' demonstrates genuine understanding of what this constituency prioritizes." },
          { name: "Understanding the 24% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "In a district this lopsidedly Republican, the 24% Democratic vote share represents a meaningful minority of voters actively seeking alternative governance — not a vanishing opposition but a significant constituency whose concerns are currently unaddressed by the Republican incumbent. Whether driven by fiscal liberalism, social policy preferences, environmental protection, or healthcare expansion, these voters represent both a vulnerability (if they mobilize) and an opportunity (if the incumbent can articulate why aspects of their agenda are better served through Republican governance). Understanding specifically who these voters are and what would move them is essential to building a genuinely durable seat rather than merely comfortable one." },
          { name: "Culture-War Messaging & Ideological Purity Tests", tag: "avoid", why: "This is an affluent, educated, professional constituency ($127,407 income, 52.8% college-educated) that evaluates candidates on policy delivery and competence, not on cultural posturing or ideological loyalty tests. Excessive emphasis on abortion, gender politics, or other culture-war themes may activate the 24% opposition without moving the existing Republican base, and risks signaling to high-income professionals that the representative prioritizes cultural grievance over fiscal management and material governance. This constituency will not reward a representative for ideological purity if it comes at the cost of education funding, property tax management, or business-friendly regulation." },
          { name: "Treating the 76% Margin as Automatic Re-Election", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76% result is commanding, but in a district with this level of income and education, it reflects voter satisfaction with the current representative's policy performance, not an unshakeable partisan base. The 6.7% renter rate means this constituency has high exit costs and low geographic mobility — they are heavily invested in this community — which means they will support a representative who delivers on their material concerns but will also organize against one who fails to do so. Treating the margin as permission to reduce constituent engagement or policy specificity is the fastest way to convert a safe seat into a contested one." }
