        memoHeadline: "Montana HD-66: A College-Educated, Young, Renter-Majority Urban District at 57% Democratic — Where Quality of Life, Housing Affordability, and Economic Mobility Drive the Margin",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MT-HD-66 is a demographically distinctive district built on human capital and urban density rather than inherited wealth or homeownership. At 62.7% college-educated — substantially above state and national norms — and with a median age of 28.8, this is a young, educated, transient constituency concentrated in rental housing (56.6%) with a median household income of $80,114. The 57% Democratic margin in 2024 reflects a coalition of young professionals, graduate students, service-sector workers, and others for whom the state's housing crisis, wage stagnation relative to cost of living, healthcare access, and the relationship between education and opportunity are not abstract policy questions but immediate material concerns. The 86.27% white demographic composition masks the district's actual economic diversity: at this age, income, and education level, the distinction between affluent professionals and financially constrained young adults navigating student debt and rental markets is far more meaningful than racial demographic categories. Understanding what 57% actually represents — which constituencies within that coalition are most volatile and what specific policy outcomes matter most to them — is the central strategic question for any candidate seeking to hold or flip this seat.",
          "The 57% result is not a stable majority; it is a plurality in a district where approximately 43% actively chose an alternative, and where the combination of youth, education, and urban density creates conditions for genuine political competition. Young, college-educated renters are often described as a reliably Democratic demographic, but that alignment is contingent on perception that Democratic governance is delivering on the issues that matter to them: housing affordability and supply, wage growth that tracks cost of living, student loan management and education affordability, healthcare access without prohibitive out-of-pocket costs, and job markets that reward their education with economic mobility. If Matthew's 57% incumbent holds this seat through perception of effective governance on these issues, a challenger who demonstrates either that the incumbent has failed on material delivery or that an alternative approach would serve these constituencies better can move the 43% opposition into viability — potentially into majority territory. The district's youth, education, and rental concentration are assets for a Democratic incumbent but also a vulnerability if younger voters conclude they are being taken for granted or if economic conditions deteriorate faster than policy responses can address them."
        ],
