          { name: "Affluent Suburban Consolidation & Higher Education Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "MT-HD-67's $91,610 median household income and 41% college attainment represent a distinctly affluent, educated suburban constituency — well above state averages and placing this district among the most economically secure in Montana. The 63-37 Republican margin suggests baseline alignment with this profile, but the 38.6% renter rate signals a meaningful cohort of educated professionals and younger adults who may prioritize quality-of-life issues (housing affordability, education funding, healthcare access) over traditional conservative positioning. Hinkle's 2024 win demonstrates viability, but holding this seat requires active engagement with educated swing voters who evaluate candidates on competence and material outcomes, not partisan label alone." },
          { name: "Younger Median Age & Non-Homeowner Economics", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 35.1-year median age and 38.6% renter rate together indicate a district where nearly four in ten households lack home equity and are exposed to rising housing costs — a material economic pressure that differentiates this from typical Republican-safe suburban seats. In an affluent district, renters are likely educated professionals or young families priced out of homeownership despite high incomes, making housing affordability and rental market stability direct constituent concerns rather than abstract policy debates. A legislative agenda that acknowledges the economic reality of younger adults and renters in an otherwise wealthy district — rather than defaulting to homeowner-centric messaging — directly addresses the constituency that generates electoral vulnerability in seats this young and this educated." },
          { name: "Educated Swing Voter Skepticism of Pure Ideology", tag: "careful", why: "College-educated voters at 41% comprise a plurality of the district and are demonstrably willing to split tickets and evaluate on competence rather than partisan loyalty — particularly when material issues (healthcare, education, housing) intersect with their lived experience. The 63-37 margin in a high-education district could mask latent Democratic strength among voters who default Republican on economics but hold reservations on social issues, institutional governance, or local service delivery. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as ideologically safe rather than as a coalition requiring active engagement with educated skeptics risks finding Democratic recruitment and turnout more robust than the 37% baseline suggests." },
          { name: "Treating Affluent Suburban Seats as Automatically Safe", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, educated suburban districts nationwide have become genuinely competitive when candidates take high-income, college-educated voters for granted or default to culture-war messaging that alienates the very professionals and families whose economic security depends on functional government and institutional stability. MT-HD-67's $91,610 income and 41% college attainment mean the constituency is economically secure enough to prioritize governance quality, education funding, and healthcare outcomes over abstract conservative principle. A representative who campaigns this seat as though wealth and education guarantee Republican loyalty — rather than as a coalition that must be actively maintained through competent constituent service and policy responsiveness — creates the conditions for credible Democratic recruitment." },
          { name: "Underestimating the Renter Constituency's Political Mobilization Potential", tag: "avoid", why: "The 38.6% renter rate in a high-income district represents educated, mobile professionals and young families who are NOT traditionally constrained by property attachment or homeowner-centric political socialization — and who, when mobilized around housing affordability, tenant protections, or rental market stability, can generate turnout and persuasion outside normal Republican assumptions. Renters in affluent suburbs are not poor voters seeking welfare; they are professionals and young families navigating housing costs that consume a larger share of income than homeowners in the same district. Campaigns that fail to directly address renter economics — and instead default to homeowner-focused property tax or development messaging — risk mobilizing precisely the 38.6% whose economic concerns are most acute and whose voting behavior is least locked into inherited partisan identity." }
