          { name: "Demonstrating Constituent Service Over Partisan Identity", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 18.3% college attainment, this constituency evaluates representatives less on ideological positioning and more on visible, tangible delivery — road maintenance, school funding, rural emergency services, property tax fairness. At 78% Republican, the incumbent can assume partisan baseline, but that baseline erodes rapidly if constituents perceive that their representative is not actively delivering on the infrastructure and service concerns that affect their daily lives. Active, visible constituent service in these concrete domains is the most durable path to maintaining the margin." },
          { name: "The ~7.79% Demographic Gap and Potential AIAN Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The six-point gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% likely represents an American Indian/Alaska Native community whose specific concerns — water rights, tribal economic development partnership, rural infrastructure equity — exist but are often overlooked in a district this heavily white and Republican. While too small to swing the district unilaterally, genuine engagement with this community on concrete governance issues (rather than token acknowledgment) builds constituent relationships and prevents a small community's grievance from becoming a mobilization point for a challenger campaign." },
          { name: "Treating 78% Republican as a Safe Majority Requiring No Active Defense", tag: "avoid", why: "The 78% result reflects partisan baseline and low Democratic organizational presence in a rural district, not deep constituent attachment to the incumbent's legislative record or vision. In a district with modest income ($55,096), aging demographics (46.5 median age), and limited education (18.3% college attainment), a challenger who runs on concrete economic grievance and constituent service can rapidly close ground. Any incumbent who assumes 78% is safe and reduces constituent engagement accordingly will find that margin eroding in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Dismissing or Minimizing Cost-of-Living Concerns as Non-Ideological", tag: "avoid", why: "Property taxes, healthcare costs, utility bills, and cost of living are not partisan issues in a district where homeowners at $55,096 income are managing tight household budgets; they are material governance concerns that transcend ideology. An incumbent who responds to cost-of-living complaints with abstract conservative rhetoric rather than concrete policy engagement — or who dismisses these concerns as not 'real' political issues — creates an opening for a challenger who takes them seriously. These concerns are the likeliest ground where a challenger can credibly contest this seat." }
