        issues: [
          { name: "Understanding the 46% Opposition in an Affluent, Educated District", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 54-46 Democratic result in a district with 51.9% college education, $79,630 income, and 75.9% homeownership is not explained by demographic inevitability — it reflects a meaningful minority of educated homeowners who preferred the Republican alternative. Whether driven by fiscal conservatism, specific education governance disputes, healthcare cost concerns, or local infrastructure disagreements, identifying and specifically addressing what generated that 46% is the single most important strategic fact about holding this seat. In an educated constituency, opposition voters are not persuadable through generalized partisan messaging but through concrete policy answers that take their concerns seriously." },
          { name: "Educated Homeowner Economic Security and Asset Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $79,630 median income, 75.9% homeownership, and 51.9% college education, MT-HD-79 is a district of professional households whose primary wealth is home equity and whose primary governance concerns are property tax fairness, education quality as a driver of home values, and infrastructure that protects their investment. Democratic policy on education funding, property tax assessment, and community development must be framed as directly protecting homeowner asset values and family economic security — not as abstract social investment. The incumbent's ability to demonstrate that Democratic fiscal policy delivers better outcomes for homeowner prosperity than Republican alternatives is essential to holding affluent educated voters." },
          { name: "College Education and Professional Opportunity Pipeline", tag: "careful", why: "With 51.9% college attainment and a 39.4-year median age, this district contains significant numbers of educated professionals and young families evaluating quality of life on education quality, job market accessibility, and professional opportunity. The Democratic incumbent must demonstrate that state education investment, higher education affordability, and workforce development policies actively support the professional opportunities that educated households depend on — not treat education as a social good in the abstract but as a direct driver of constituent economic mobility and career trajectory." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 result in any district is a plurality of contested voters, not a mandate — and in an affluent, educated constituency where voters are capable of sophisticated evaluation of policy alternatives, assuming that the 54% represents a stable coalition is strategically dangerous. The opposition is substantial and educated enough to mount a credible alternative if the incumbent appears indifferent to their material concerns. This is a seat that demands active constituent engagement on the specific issues driving the opposition, not the complacency of treating 54% as a durable floor." },
          { name: "Assuming Affluent Voters Are Naturally Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated, affluent homeowners in Montana are not a reliably Democratic constituency — they evaluate on fiscal responsibility, education quality, and whether government is delivering material value for their tax dollars. Any Democratic incumbent in MT-HD-79 who campaigns as though affluent educated voters should naturally align with Democratic positions on principle, rather than demonstrating specific policy delivery on homeowner priorities, will find that assumption actively hostile to voter behavior in this district. Affluence and education increase sophistication in evaluating policy alternatives, not partisan loyalty." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "MT-HD-79: A College-Educated, Affluent Swing District at 54% Democratic — the Incumbent's Margin Depends on Holding Educated Homeowners and Understanding Why 46% Chose Opposition",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MT-HD-79 is a genuinely affluent district with a college-educated majority (51.9%), a median household income of $79,630, and a 39.4-year median age that signals a settled, asset-holding constituency. The 24.1% renter rate indicates a strong homeowner base, and the 90.79% white demographic composition reflects a homogeneous community with minimal racial diversity. The 54% Democratic result in 2024 is the critical strategic fact: this is not a safely blue district in a red state, but rather a swing district where the incumbent Democrat (Muszkiewicz) won by only four percentage points. In a district this affluent and educated, that 46% opposition is not accidental — it reflects specific policy disagreements or governance concerns among a constituency educated enough to evaluate candidates on substance and wealthy enough to have material interests at stake.",
          "The 54-46 margin in an affluent, college-educated, homeowner-majority district is unstable without active engagement with the concerns driving the opposition. Educated homeowners at the $79,630 income level with college degrees evaluate on concrete policy outcomes: property tax fairness, education funding quality, healthcare costs, and economic development that protects their asset values and professional opportunities. The relatively small margin suggests that Muszkiewicz's coalition includes most college-educated voters and a decisive share of homeowners, but that a meaningful minority of both groups preferred the Republican alternative — likely on fiscal concerns, education governance, or specific local issues. The incumbent's path to consolidating this seat and building toward a durable majority involves identifying specifically what drove the 46%, addressing those concerns with credible policy commitments, and ensuring that the educated homeowner majority sees the Democratic incumbent as the candidate most likely to deliver on their material priorities. Neglecting this analysis and treating 54% as a safe floor is the fastest path to losing a seat that should be held."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with education quality and K-12 funding as a concrete policy priority — a 51.9% college-educated district with a 39.4-year median age contains substantial parents evaluating schools as a primary household investment; be specific about how Democratic education policy delivers better outcomes for this constituency than opposition alternatives.",
          "Engage the homeowner majority directly on property tax burden and assessment fairness — at $79,630 median income and 75.9% homeownership, property tax as a percentage of household wealth is a material concern for this constituency; demonstrate that Democratic fiscal policy protects homeowner economic security better than Republican alternatives.",
          "Address the 46% opposition with specificity: identify whether it concentrates around fiscal conservatism, specific local governance decisions, education disputes, or healthcare costs — and offer concrete answers that take the opposition's concerns seriously rather than dismissing them as ideological or illegitimate.",
          "Build coalition durability by connecting college education to economic opportunity in this region — young professionals and educated families choose where to live based on education quality, job markets, healthcare access, and community stability; ensure Democratic messaging connects education funding and professional opportunity as integrated priorities."
        ]
