          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Economic Security & Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 89.7% homeownership and the highest median income in this district sequence, MT-HD-80 voters have significant financial stakes in property tax policy, housing affordability for their adult children, and whether state government is managing fiscal resources in ways that protect household wealth. The 54-46 margin is narrow enough that a Republican challenger who credibly frames property tax fairness and fiscal discipline can peel away persuadable homeowners concerned about cost of living and local tax burden. Romano must proactively address homeowner economic concerns with specific, tangible policy commitments rather than ceding tax and fiscal messaging to the opposition." },
          { name: "Government Efficiency & Return-on-Investment for Tax Dollars", tag: "careful", why: "An affluent, college-educated constituency at $96,005 income evaluates government on competence and measurable results — not partisan loyalty. If state agencies are perceived as inefficient, if education funding is not translating into better outcomes, or if infrastructure investment is not delivering visible community benefits, persuadable professionals and entrepreneurs will consider voting for an alternative who promises better stewardship. The 54-46 margin suggests this evaluation is active and contested; Romano must demonstrate concrete, measurable results from state investment, not rely on Democratic partisan identity to hold skeptical, high-income voters." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Safe Democratic Stronghold", tag: "avoid", why: "A 4-point margin in an affluent, college-educated, persuadable district is not a safe hold — it is a competitive seat that depends on active constituent engagement and measurable delivery on core constituency concerns. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 54% represents a floor rather than a contested margin will be vulnerable to a Republican challenger who speaks credibly about fiscal discipline and government efficiency. The college-educated homeowner constituency is not inherently Democratic; it is persuadable based on whether government is delivering competence and results." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 5.66% Demographic Gap or Rural Community Dynamics", tag: "avoid", why: "While smaller than in many rural Montana districts, the 5.66% gap between reported Census categories and 100% represents a portion of the district's population whose needs and concerns may not align with the college-educated, affluent professional profile. Rural agricultural families, small-town business owners, or regional economic clusters not fully captured in the standard Census categories have distinct governance concerns around agricultural policy, rural infrastructure, and economic development. A legislative agenda that addresses only the affluent professional base while neglecting these other constituents will miss opportunities to broaden support or may face unaddressed opposition that grows over a cycle." }
