          { name: "College-Educated, Higher-Income Suburban Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46.6% college-educated and $67,840 median income, MT-HD-81 is an above-average education and income district that has consolidated decisively around the Democratic incumbent's 56% margin. This is a constituency responsive to messaging around education investment, professional workforce development, and quality-of-life governance that treats higher-education and higher-income households as the district's demographic core. The 39.1% renter rate signals younger professionals and graduate-degree holders for whom housing affordability, student debt, and professional opportunity are material concerns; a representative who speaks fluently to these specific economic pressures of educated, working professionals commands the credibility this cohort expects." },
          { name: "Younger Adult Median Age & Renter Population Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 37.8 median age and 39.1% renter rate, MT-HD-81 is a notably younger, more renter-heavy district than typical Republican-leaning rural Montana. This signals a constituency of younger professionals, graduate students, and early-career households for whom housing costs, wages relative to cost of living, and the economic ladder from entry-level to mid-career stability are the primary quality-of-life concerns. The 56% Democratic result reflects this cohort's evaluation that current governance is addressing their material needs; any Republican challenger must offer concrete, specific policy remedies to housing affordability, wage growth, or workforce opportunity that directly compete with the incumbent's positioning rather than defaulting to abstract conservative messaging." },
          { name: "The 44% Republican Opposition in an Educated, Higher-Income District", tag: "careful", why: "Even with a 56-44 Democratic margin, MT-HD-81 retains 44% Republican support in a district with above-average college attainment and income — demographic categories that in many Montana districts lean Republican on fiscal and governance grounds. This opposition likely includes college-educated professionals and higher-income homeowners for whom tax policy, regulatory burden, and business-friendly governance are the primary electoral drivers, suggesting that the Republican base here is not merely ideological but tethered to specific economic interests. Understanding what holds or could shift these educated, higher-income Republican voters — whether property tax policy, small-business regulation, or skepticism about particular Democratic spending priorities — is essential to understanding both the incumbent's vulnerability and a challenger's opening." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Durable Majority Rather Than as a Contingent Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result in a district with a younger, renter-heavy population and educated workforce is not a commanding majority — it is a coalition of younger professionals, college-educated voters, and working-class renters whose continued support depends on perceiving concrete returns from incumbent governance. If housing costs continue to rise faster than wages, if rental availability tightens, or if educated voters perceive regulatory or tax policy as unfavorable to professional opportunity, this coalition can fracture. An incumbent who campaigns as though 56% represents a stable floor rather than a coalition requiring active maintenance and delivery of tangible results will discover too late that the margin was conditional, not structural." },
          { name: "Allowing the 5.2% Hispanic Community to Remain Invisible in Campaign Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "With 5.2% Hispanic population and 88% white, MT-HD-81 is a heavily white district where the Hispanic community can easily be treated as a negligible constituency — but at 5.2%, this cohort represents roughly 900-1000 households in a district where the incumbent won by 12 percentage points. In younger, college-educated, renter-heavy districts with professional economies, Hispanic workers are often concentrated in service-sector, construction, and healthcare roles with distinct concerns around workplace safety, wage enforcement, and housing accessibility that differ from the broader educated professional constituency. Ignoring this community's material needs — or assuming they will vote Democratic regardless of campaign attention — cedes the opportunity to build specific constituent relationships and leaves an opening for a Republican challenger to offer targeted outreach around economic mobility and community safety." }
