          { name: "Education Quality and the College-Educated Base", tag: "careful", why: "With 30.4% college attainment — above state average — MT-HD-88 has a meaningfully educated constituency that invests heavily in K-12 education quality and views education as a path to opportunity, not a culture-war battleground. This is a constituency that expects its representative to defend education funding and quality without partisan theatre, but also a constituency where sustained culture-war messaging on schools can create distance from the incumbent if perceived as prioritizing ideology over educational outcomes. The challenge is that education is simultaneously a core constituent interest and a domain where partisan messaging has become increasingly aggressive — requiring a candidate to thread the needle between taking education seriously and refusing to weaponize it." },
          { name: "Treating the 70% as a Stable, Earned Landslide Rather Than a Vulnerable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "Overstreet's 70% is substantial, but it is held by a constituency — college-educated, affluent, older homeowners — that has become less reliably Republican over the past four cycles nationally. At 30% Democratic in a district with 86% white population and $80k+ income, the opposition share is higher than historical norms, which indicates incremental movement rather than sudden collapse, but movement nonetheless. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 70% is a durable baseline rather than a coalition requiring active constituent attention to material interests will find that coalition more vulnerable in the next challenging cycle than the raw 2024 margin suggests." },
          { name: "Pursuing Culture-War or Social Issue Positioning as a Primary Agenda", tag: "avoid", why: "While MT-HD-88 is conservative on the balance, its college-educated demographic (30.4%) and affluent profile ($80k+ income) make this a constituency for which social issues and culture-war positioning are secondary to material governance concerns: property tax fairness, healthcare sustainability, education quality, and environmental stewardship. Any candidate — particularly an incumbent — who leads with or prioritizes culture-war messaging, transgender issues, or education battles over the material concerns of property owners and older adults will create distance from this constituency's core interests and provide an opening for a challenger to position as defending what voters actually care about." }
