          { name: "Renter Majority & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 47.7% renter rate — nearly half the district — is the single most important demographic vulnerability for a Republican in an affluent, college-educated district. Renters in a $74,781 median-income area are likely experiencing real housing cost pressure, and their economic interests diverge sharply from homeowner-majority districts where property tax and appreciation concerns dominate. A legislator who is silent on housing affordability, renter protections, or zoning reform is ceding this constituency to whatever opposition can articulate a credible housing-cost message. This is the demographic segment most likely to be persuadable away from the incumbent and most likely to mobilize a Democratic challenger." },
          { name: "Democratic Organizational Absence & Vulnerable Unopposed Status", tag: "careful", why: "Cochran's unopposed status in a 57.1% college-educated, 47.7% renter, affluent professional district is a warning signal, not a vindication. It reflects Democratic organizational decisions, not durable voter preference or a consolidated Republican advantage. The moment Democratic Party resources target this district — which is likely if the state party rebuilds organizational capacity in suburban areas — the unopposed outcome will reverse, and the incumbent will face a credible challenger on terrain (housing, healthcare, education funding) where the district's demographic composition favors the opposition. Any incumbent who interprets the unopposed result as permission to neglect constituent engagement is misreading the strategic environment." },
          { name: "Running as an Ideological Conservative Rather Than a District Representative", tag: "avoid", why: "MT-HD-90's college-educated, younger, renter-heavy demographic composition is fundamentally misaligned with a campaign centered on abstract ideological conservatism or state-level culture-war positioning. This constituency evaluates on whether government is solving their specific economic problems — housing costs, healthcare access, infrastructure quality — not whether the legislator is rhetorically committed to shrinking government or pursuing ideological priorities orthogonal to district life. Any candidate who leads with ideological positioning rather than district-specific governance concerns is conceding the territory where this district's persuadable voters actually live." },
          { name: "Assuming the Unopposed Outcome Signals Durable Support", tag: "avoid", why: "The most dangerous strategic error an incumbent can make in this district is treating the unopposed result as evidence of entrenched support rather than as a temporary organizational advantage. In a district where 57.1% are college-educated, 47.7% are renters, and the median age is 35, the constituency is highly responsive to quality-of-life governance and unusually susceptible to mobilization by a credible alternative. The unopposed outcome creates a window for the incumbent to build a visible, constituent-responsive legislative record — but only if that window is used actively. Passive assumption that the opposition will remain absent is the path to a competitive race the moment a challenger emerges." }
