          { name: "Homeowner Economic Stability & Property Stewardship", tag: "lean-into", why: "With an 81.6% homeownership rate, MT-HD-92 is fundamentally a district of asset-holding households whose economic security depends on property values, neighborhood quality, and stable local services. The 44.8-year median age and $66,757 income profile a constituency in mid-career with significant equity at stake and limited appetite for radical change or policies perceived as destabilizing to residential character. Keogh's 61% margin reflects solid performance among this cohort; sustaining the seat requires active engagement on property tax management, school quality as a homeowner concern, and land-use decisions that protect neighborhood stability rather than abstract ideological positioning." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity & Minority Community Invisibility", tag: "careful", why: "At 84.4% white with Hispanic, Black, and Asian communities each representing less than 4% of the population, MT-HD-92 is one of Montana's most demographically homogeneous districts — and the four reported categories account for approximately 89% of the population, leaving roughly 11% unaccounted for, likely American Indian residents. While the district's electoral alignment is strongly Democratic at 61%, the absence of visible minority community engagement in campaign messaging or constituent services creates a governance blind spot: any Asian, Hispanic, Black, or Native American resident who lives in MT-HD-92 is effectively invisible in most standard campaign narratives. This is not primarily an electoral vulnerability at current margins, but it is a representation failure and a latent signal that minority constituents may not feel served." },
          { name: "Taking 61% as a Sustainable Floor Rather Than a Contingent Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61% result in a midterm or presidential cycle is not automatically reproducible; it reflects Keogh's specific alignment with the district's educated professional and homeowner constituencies at this moment. Any incumbent or challenger who assumes 61% represents a stable Democratic base — rather than a result contingent on active engagement with the college-educated professionals and property owners who comprise it — is arithmetically vulnerable. The 39% Republican share in a district this educated and affluent is not insignificant; it signals meaningful conservative sentiment among homeowners and professionals on issues like regulation, government spending, or development policy that can shift under the right candidate or circumstances." },
          { name: "Neglecting Rural or Non-Professional Working-Class Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "MT-HD-92's 30.5% college attainment rate means 69.5% of adults lack a four-year degree — a substantial working-class and service-sector constituency whose needs (wage adequacy, healthcare access, job training, affordable housing) can become invisible when the dominant campaign narrative focuses on professional concerns. The 18.4% renter rate signals a minority but meaningful population of non-homeowners for whom housing costs and tenant protections are material issues; treating the district as exclusively a professional-homeowner conversation alienates this constituency and leaves it available to opposition mobilization. A representative who speaks only to the college-educated 30.5% is governing less than one-third of the actual district." }
