          { name: "Attempting to Move the Republican 20%", tag: "avoid", why: "In an 80-20 Democratic district, the Republican 20% represents a consolidated ideological minority that is unlikely to move on candidate quality or local conditions alone. Resources spent attempting to appeal to this Republican core are resources not spent deepening Democratic turnout, consolidating renter and young-adult economic messaging, or engaging the college-educated plurality on education and opportunity issues where real coalition-building occurs. The strategic math in a supermajority district favors consolidation of the existing coalition, not pursuit of marginal Republican votes." },
          { name: "Treating the 80% Margin as Permanent or Requiring Minimal Effort", tag: "avoid", why: "While the demographic profile supports a strong Democratic result, an 80-20 margin depends on active activation of a constituency — renters, young adults, college-educated professionals — that has lower baseline turnout and higher geographic mobility than older homeowner-dominant districts. Any incumbent or challenger who assumes the 80% is secure and campaigns without specific emphasis on housing, education, and young-adult economic security is operating on false confidence. This is a safe seat for Democrats whose agenda addresses the material concerns of this specific constituency, not a seat that holds without engagement." }
