          "MT-HD-99 is the bluest district in deep-red Montana — a 56-44 Democratic result that reflects the University of Montana's economic and cultural footprint in Missoula and a college-educated professional constituency concentrated in education, healthcare, research, and knowledge-sector employment. The 45.8% college attainment rate and $89,127 median household income are the two most important analytical facts about the district: this is not a working-class Democratic base, but rather an educated, asset-holding constituency that evaluates candidates on their economic competence and quality-of-life track record. France's 56% margin was built on consolidating college-educated voters and educated professionals concerned about education funding, healthcare access, and quality-of-life amenities that retain talent and young professionals in western Montana. Any challenge to this seat must either break significant ground among college-educated voters — a difficult lift — or convince homeowners that a Republican candidate offers superior economic management on taxes, housing costs, and fiscal stewardship.",
          "The underlying tension in MT-HD-99 is the relationship between income and housing affordability. At $89,127 median household income with 67.2% homeownership, this is objectively a prosperous community — but western Montana's housing cost inflation over the past five years has compressed disposable income even for above-average earners, and the squeeze is acute for homeowners managing property taxes, insurance, and maintenance on fixed or modest-growth incomes. The 32.8% renter rate signals a college-town dynamic where young professionals and service workers rent while the homeowner-majority ages in place; this creates two distinct economic constituencies with different leverage and different concerns. A candidate who can speak to housing affordability constraints even at above-average income levels, and who offers specific remedies around property tax policy or housing supply, builds credibility with the prosperous-but-squeezed homeowners who represent France's margin. The 44% Republican opposition in this educated, high-income district suggests that some voters believe a different economic approach — whether property tax relief, reduced regulation, or fiscal restraint — would better serve their material interests; dismissing that 44% as ideologically fixed rather than persuadable misreads the district's actual political economy."
