          "The strategic challenge in MT-HD-100 is not about recapturing opposition territory — it is about ensuring that the 78% supermajority reflects genuine constituent engagement rather than passive partisan alignment. In a district where 59.3% of residents rent rather than own, housing affordability, tenant protections, and the relationship between residential stability and economic mobility are material governance questions, not ideological abstractions. The college-educated, young, urban constituency votes Democratic at supermajority levels, but their vote is contingent on material delivery: transit access, public education quality, housing cost management, and labor market connectivity. The 22% opposition in an urban district this young and educated likely reflects either specific local governance failures, economic grievances among renters or service-sector workers, or defection among college-educated voters on fiscal or housing-policy grounds. Understanding who that 22% is and why they rejected the incumbent is the most important diagnostic work the holder of this seat can do — because at 78%, the only direction the margin can move is down."
