          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access & Aging Community Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 47.3 median age indicates a constituency aging into healthcare cost sensitivity and service accessibility concerns that pure tax conservatism does not address. Rural hospital viability, Medicare sustainability, and the cost and accessibility of healthcare services for older adults are material governance concerns for a district where demographic trends are shifting toward fixed-income constraints. A legislative agenda that addresses these concerns with specificity — rather than defaulting to abstract fiscal messaging — better serves the aging majority and insulates against challenger messaging about whether current governance is actually delivering for older constituents." },
          { name: "Income Variation & Less-Affluent Homeowner Constraints", tag: "careful", why: "While the $71,971 median income and 47.2% college education suggest overall affluence, the 52.8% without a college degree and income distribution variation within the homeowner base indicate a plurality facing real economic constraint. A challenger who can credibly argue that current governance is not addressing healthcare costs, service accessibility, or economic opportunity for the less-affluent portion of the homeowner coalition could peel support from the 70% Republican base. The incumbent must maintain specific constituent attention to this group's material concerns to prevent erosion." },
          { name: "Partisan Realignment Through Education Gaps", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 47.2% college education rate is high and typically correlates with Republican strength in rural areas, national trends show college-educated voters increasingly sensitive to healthcare policy, environmental governance, and social issue positioning. Any candidate who assumes that education level automatically translates to stable Republican support — rather than remaining attentive to how educated voters evaluate governance on healthcare, environmental sustainability, and institutional competence — risks losing the coalition's most mobile voters to a credible challenger positioned on these issues." },
          { name: "Treating 70-30 as a Safe Majority Rather Than a Coalition to Maintain", tag: "avoid", why: "While 70-30 is a commanding result, it represents the alignment of a specific demographic coalition — affluent, educated, older, homeowner-dominant — whose material concerns are healthcare cost, service accessibility, property tax fairness, and governance stability. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though the 70% margin represents a stable base rather than an active coalition to be maintained will find that attention to governing these constituents' actual concerns erodes, and a credible challenger positioned on healthcare or service delivery can crack the margin." }
