          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access & Cost", tag: "lean-into", why: "MT-SD-4's $64,413 median income and working-class composition place healthcare costs at or near the top of household budget pressures. Rural hospital viability, IHS access and quality for the ~9% Native American population, Medicaid expansion and coverage gaps, and the rural physician shortage are not abstract policy questions—they are direct economic and health security concerns for constituents who cannot absorb healthcare shocks. A representative with a concrete legislative record on rural healthcare delivery and investment is addressing a material constituent priority and differentiating from challengers who offer only partisan rhetoric on healthcare." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Working-Class Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 39.3% of households renting and a 34-year median age, MT-SD-4 contains a significant cohort of younger adults and working families for whom housing costs are competing directly with other essential expenses. The median income of $64,413 is insufficient to absorb rapid rental or property tax increases, and a 30.1% college attainment rate suggests limited wage-premium pathways to economic mobility. A candidate who campaigns on housing affordability, property tax relief, or rental assistance is addressing a material priority for the district's non-homeowner and younger-adult constituencies and differentiating from competitors who default to abstract economic messaging." },
          { name: "Native American Community Civic Infrastructure & Tribal Governance Partnership", tag: "careful", why: "The ~9% AIAN gap in the demographic data represents a population whose civic organization, healthcare relationships, and political preferences are shaped by tribal nations and Indian Health Service structures rather than purely by partisan messaging. Tribal governments, IHS, and Native American community leaders have the capacity to mobilize their constituencies around specific governance priorities—sovereignty, healthcare access, economic development—and at 8-9% of the district's population, this community is large enough that modest shifts in turnout or candidate preference could materially alter the margin. A representative perceived as engaged with tribal nations and Native American economic concerns will retain this constituency; one perceived as indifferent risks seeing it mobilized by a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Abstract Conservative Ideology Without Working-Class Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 margin is not a license to govern on partisan principle alone; it reflects a working-class and renting constituency that evaluates elected officials on whether they are delivering tangible improvements in cost of living, healthcare access, and economic security. Any candidate who campaigns on abstract conservatism—lower taxes, less regulation, traditional values—without addressing the material economic pressures in a district where nearly 40% of households rent and median income is below $65,000 risks allowing an opposition candidate to capture the economic-security message and activate latent opposition among younger adults and the Native American community." },
          { name: "Assuming the AIAN Gap Is Politically Inert", tag: "avoid", why: "The ~9% of MT-SD-4's population not captured in the four reported Census categories is not a null political factor; it is a community served by tribal governments, Indian Health Service, and Native American civic institutions with the organizational capacity to mobilize voters around specific issues—tribal sovereignty, healthcare access, economic development—and to coordinate endorsements and turnout. Any incumbent or challenger who assumes this gap represents a politically passive or naturally Republican population is miscalculating the district's actual political structure. The margin is durable only if tribal leadership and Native American constituents perceive the representative as engaged with their priorities; otherwise, the gap represents latent opposition capacity." }
