          "Understand the 26% opposition: determine whether Democratic gains concentrate among younger college-educated adults (whose economic priorities may include healthcare access, education funding, and environmental stewardship) or among the ~6.5% demographic gap (which may include American Indian residents with distinct tribal governance and healthcare concerns). The 74% margin is substantial but not invulnerable if opposition is organized around specific constituent concerns rather than generic partisanship.",
          "Maintain consistent geographic presence and constituent accessibility: a dispersed rural district with low population density requires active, personal engagement with local communities, agricultural organizations, and civic institutions. A supermajority margin can mask atrophy in constituent relationships if an incumbent becomes too comfortable with the numbers.",
          "Address the economic variance within the median: while $76,487 is above-median for rural Montana, the district likely contains both prosperous agricultural operations and fixed-income retirees or younger working families for whom that median masks real economic stress. Targeted engagement on rural healthcare access, property tax relief, and cost-of-living pressures for lower-income households within the district prevents Democratic messaging from gaining traction on economic security."
