          { name: "Treating 73% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73% margin in a district with a 51.8-year median age and 18.3% renter rate may reflect genuine constituent satisfaction with the incumbent, but it also reflects a demographic profile vulnerable to economic disruption — older homeowners with fixed incomes are acutely sensitive to changes in property taxes, healthcare costs, or rural service cuts. Any representative who campaigns this seat as though 73% represents an insurmountable advantage, rather than consolidating it through active constituent engagement on aging-household economic security and rural infrastructure, risks discovering that margin is more fragile than it appears if economic conditions shift or a challenger emerges with credibility on these issues." },
          { name: "College Education as a Class Dividing Line", tag: "avoid", why: "The 24.1% college attainment rate is substantially lower than Montana's state average, and framing education policy through an urban professional lens — student debt relief, urban university funding, or tech-sector workforce development — will misalign with the lived experience of a rural, aging, non-college-educated majority. Any candidate who positions college education as a marker of status or merit relative to the working-class, trade-skilled, or agricultural majority in MT-SD-7 will find that framing actively alienating to a constituency that has built stable lives without four-year degrees. Education policy should be grounded in rural vocational pathways, skilled trades training, and K-12 adequacy — not college completion as the primary measure of educational value." }
