          { name: "Education Quality & Higher Education Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 36.6% college attainment — above Montana's statewide average — MT-SD-10 is an educated constituency that evaluates candidates on specific education policy performance. This includes both K-12 school quality (directly affecting property values and family decisions) and whether state higher education and workforce development policy is preparing the next generation for economic opportunity. The 48% Democratic share in an affluent, homeowner-dominant district suggests that a meaningful portion of the educated constituency perceives current education investment as insufficient or misaligned with their children's needs. Concrete proposals on school funding levels, teacher compensation, and the state's role in post-secondary education access are the substance this constituency requires." },
          { name: "Tribal & AIAN Community Representation", tag: "careful", why: "The approximately 4.77 percentage point gap between reported Census categories and 100% likely represents American Indian or Alaska Native residents whose political participation in Montana close races has demonstrated significant swing capacity. In a 52-48 seat, the turnout and voting behavior of this constituency — which may comprise several thousand voters — is mathematically relevant to the outcome, and their governance expectations regarding tribal sovereignty, healthcare access, and economic development are legitimate constituent concerns. Any candidate who treats this district as solely an affluent Anglo homeowner community is failing to represent the full constituency and leaving electoral engagement on the table." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A four-point margin in a district this educated and affluent is not a durable majority — it is the outcome of the last campaign and the incumbent's specific performance record. Voters in a 36.6% college-educated, $85,703 median income constituency are capable of evaluating and changing their preferences based on concrete policy performance; this is not a region where partisan loyalty is inherited or fixed. Any candidate who approaches this seat with the confidence that 52% represents a stable floor, rather than a result that must be actively defended through engagement with the 48% opposition, is strategically vulnerable." },
          { name: "Generic Conservative Messaging in an Education-Driven Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "Abstract conservative messaging about government overreach or tax reduction does not resonate with an affluent, educated, homeowner-dominant constituency that is evaluating candidates on specific policy questions affecting their material interests. In a district where 36.6% of adults hold college degrees and median household income is $85,703, voters expect substantive, detailed policy proposals — not partisan slogans. Generic conservative positioning that ignores the specific education and tax policy concerns driving the 48% opposition will fail to consolidate the existing Republican base and will continue to bleed support to Democratic candidates offering more concrete policy engagement." }
