          "The district's economic baseline is the strategic fulcrum for both defense and attack. At $52,402 median income—below Montana's state median and well below the national median—household budgets are constrained by rising housing, healthcare, and utility costs. For the 57.8% who own homes, property tax increases, home insurance costs, and maintenance pressures on aging housing stock are direct financial concerns. For the 42.2% who rent, housing affordability and wage stagnation relative to rental cost increases are the primary material grievance. A Republican incumbent holds this seat by consolidating homeowner support around property rights and tax messaging while avoiding specificity on renter-household economic security or the concrete costs renters face. A Democratic challenger's most efficient path to a 50%+ result does not require persuading homeowners to switch parties—it requires mobilizing renters, younger adults (median age 37.4), and the 13% demographic gap population to turnout at levels closer to homeowner participation, then offering concrete renter-household economic answers (rental assistance, wage floors, utilities cost relief) that address the material concerns the incumbent is not meeting. Neither party can afford to treat the renter constituency as secondary or symbolic; at 42.2%, they are the core swing population."
