          { name: "Native American Community Engagement & Water Rights Partnership", tag: "lean-into", why: "The approximately 7% demographic gap likely represents Native American residents whose electoral alignment with the Republican coalition is neither secure nor articulated in current constituent service models. Water rights, healthcare access through tribal systems, and tribal economic development are the governance frameworks through which this population evaluates state-level representation, and a representative who engages these issues with institutional fluency and genuine partnership builds durable support beyond the current 71%. Treating this population as politically inert rather than as a legitimate constituent group with specific governance needs leaves both electoral opportunity and governing capacity unused." },
          { name: "Understanding Opposition Concentration", tag: "careful", why: "The 29% Democratic share in a heavily Republican rural district may be concentrated among specific constituencies—renters, younger adults, or the Native American population—whose distinct concerns are being underserved by current representation. Identifying whether opposition is driven by economic anxiety, specific governance failures on rural services, or deliberate political organization in tribal communities allows a candidate to either defend against it with targeted constituent service or to treat it as noise depending on its actual source. In a district this partisan, understanding the 29% is more strategically important than consolidating the existing 71%." },
          { name: "Treating 71% as a Mandate to Ignore Minority Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 71% margin does not authorize representative to treat the remaining 29%—and the ~7% of the population in the demographic gap—as irrelevant to governance. The homeowner majority is real and stable, but the Native American population and any renters or younger adults in the opposition are constituent groups with legitimate governance needs that remain independent of partisan preference. A representative who campaigns and governs as though 71% is permission to ignore or minimize constituent service to opposition communities will find that margin eroding as the neglected population organizes alternatives." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Over Pragmatic Problem-Solving", tag: "avoid", why: "At 22.4% college attainment and $66,563 income, MT-SD-20 is a constituency that evaluates representatives on whether they deliver concrete results on material concerns—not on the coherence of their ideological positioning or partisan messaging. A candidate who leads with abstract conservative rhetoric about government overreach rather than specific legislative solutions to rural hospital closures, water rights, property tax fairness, or healthcare costs is misreading the district's actual constituency and wasting opportunity to build durable support among homeowners who care primarily about whether their representative is solving their problems." }
