          { name: "Younger Adult Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 32.9 median age and 34.8% renter rate, MT-SD-21's swing constituency is working-age adults under direct financial pressure from housing costs and employment volatility at $56,290 median household income. This younger, more mobile constituency evaluates candidates on whether government is delivering material improvement to their economic opportunity—not inherited partisan identity. Housing policy, workforce development, and healthcare access are the concrete issues this demographic bloc cares about, and they represent a credible path to 50%+ if mobilized by a candidate offering genuine solutions." },
          { name: "Diagnosing the 43% Opposition in a Majority-Native American District", tag: "careful", why: "The 43% Democratic result in a district that is roughly 66% Native American and 35% young renters is neither anomalous nor permanent—it reflects baseline opposition concentrated among two overlapping constituencies that any challenger can mobilize further if they offer authentic engagement. Understanding whether the opposition is driven primarily by Native American voters mobilized around tribal sovereignty, by renters frustrated with housing and healthcare policy, by younger adults evaluating economic opportunity, or by some combination is the diagnostic prerequisite to knowing whether 57% is a durable margin or a vulnerability waiting to be triggered." },
          { name: "Treating the Demographic Majority as a Data Footnote", tag: "avoid", why: "The ~66% demographic gap is not a minor Census artifact—it represents the actual majority population of MT-SD-21, most likely Native American residents whose governance needs, economic concerns, and political mobilization capacity are central to any sustainable electoral coalition. Any legislator who conducts constituent service, writes legislative priorities, or allocates staff attention as if the 34% captured in the four standard categories represents the district's actual priority is governing a phantom district, not the one that actually exists, and will find that governance failure reflected in electoral vulnerability when opposition mobilizes the demographic majority." },
          { name: "Assuming 57% Represents a Structural Republican Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57% result in a district this young (32.9 median age), this high-renter (34.8%), this economically stressed ($56,290 income), and this demographically complex (~66% not captured in standard categories) is not a floor—it is a performance outcome that depends on active constituent service to both the existing base and enough swing voters to prevent organized opposition mobilization. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 57% is a safe majority, or any challenger who assumes it is unbeatable, is making a strategic error; at 32.9 median age and majority-Native American population, this district has the demographic and economic profile of a swing seat that can move decisively if either candidate fails to deliver or the opposition organizes effectively." }
