        memoHeadline: "Southwestern Montana Rural Working Class: 66% Republican, $69K Income, Healthcare Access as the Core Economic Security Issue, and One-Third Opposition Not Yet Consolidated",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MT-SD-22 is a solidly Republican rural district in southwestern Montana where the incumbent won 66% in 2022 — a substantial but not overwhelming margin in a region where rural voters increasingly evaluate candidates on whether government delivers material results. The district's demographic profile is straightforward: 84.48% white, 23.4% renter rate, and a 37.1-year median age that reflects a working-class, homeowner-dominant, family-oriented constituency. The $69,481 median household income is the critical fact: this is not a wealthy district, and in an inflationary environment where healthcare costs, housing affordability, and the viability of small-town economies are under pressure, that income level means households are making real trade-offs between competing financial demands. The 25.6% college attainment rate suggests limited economic mobility through education, which means the district's economic future depends on viable local employment — agriculture, small business, and whatever service or professional sectors can sustain themselves in southwestern Montana geography. The 66% result is solid, but the 34% opposition in a Republican-lean state suggests that one-third of the district's voters are either dissatisfied with the incumbent's performance or persuadable by an alternative who speaks specifically to their economic concerns.",
          "For an incumbent or challenger in MT-SD-22, the primary strategic fact is that rural voters in this income range are pragmatists first and partisans second. They care whether their healthcare is accessible and affordable, whether their property values and home equity are secure, whether their kids have reason to stay in southwestern Montana, and whether state government is investing in the infrastructure — rural hospitals, broadband, roads, water systems — that makes their community viable. The ~9% demographic gap (beyond the four reported Census categories) likely includes American Indian / Alaska Native constituents, particularly those connected to tribal territories in the region; engaging this population authentically on healthcare access, economic development, and state-tribal cooperation is both a governance obligation and a signal to the broader rural community that the representative takes constituent needs seriously rather than assuming partisan loyalty. A candidate who wins or holds MT-SD-22 must demonstrate specific legislative accomplishments on rural healthcare financing, cost-of-living pressures, and rural infrastructure investment — not abstract conservative positioning. The margin is solid, but the 34% opposition and the underlying economic pressures on a $69,481-income constituency suggest this is a seat that demands active constituent service and tangible results, not quiet confidence in partisan lean."
        ],
