          "The true strategic vulnerability of MT-SD-29 is that its 55% Republican margin rests on an older homeowner base whose material concerns — healthcare costs, property tax burden, rural service viability, and fixed-income security in an inflationary environment — can easily drift if a challenger offers concrete answers to those pressures more persuasively than the incumbent. At $76,491 median household income and 49.1 median age, this constituency is asset-rich but income-constrained on many items critical to quality of life; healthcare cost escalation and property tax policy are not abstract concerns but direct household budget questions. The 22.7% renter population and younger adults in the district represent a modest but real swing constituency that could be mobilized around housing affordability, healthcare access, or economic opportunity if a challenger campaigns directly to their concerns rather than assuming they are locked into a homeowner-weighted Republican majority. A 55-45 margin in a district this affluent and this white is not politically soft in Montana's current environment, but it is not impervious to a challenger who understands that the incumbent's 55% is primarily a homeowner-majority coalition and that the remaining 45% includes both disaffected older voters and younger adults with distinct material concerns."
