          { name: "Assuming Affluence Produces Partisan Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "MT-SD-31's high income ($107,557) and education (71% college-educated) might be expected to produce a strong Republican result, but instead produced a 52-48 margin. This gap between expected and actual outcomes suggests that education and affluence, in this particular district, do not automatically translate into partisan loyalty. Candidates who assume that college-educated, affluent voters in Montana will default to their party's broader positioning — without specific engagement on the issues that matter to this particular educated, affluent constituency — will find themselves surprised by defections. This district requires individualized candidate-to-voter persuasion, not reliance on demographic stereotypes." }
