          "The low college-attainment rate (22.6%) is the single most important demographic signal. This is not a credential-focused, knowledge-worker district. It is a place where people work in trades, manufacturing, services, and extraction-adjacent industries. Educational messaging must center on skills, apprenticeships, and immediate economic return—not degrees for their own sake. The renter supermajority (36.6%) is politically volatile: renters are more responsive to housing costs, less insulated from economic shocks, and more likely to swing based on felt material conditions. Hold this district by delivering tangible improvements in wages, housing affordability, and job quality. Lose it by appearing indifferent to economic pain or by allowing the opposition to reframe economics as secondary to cultural conflict."
