          { name: "Property Owner Economic Security & Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $74,901 median income with 82.1% homeownership and 44.1-year median age, MT-SD-38 is a community of property-holding adults for whom tax burden, infrastructure maintenance, and whether government delivers efficient service are the primary governance concerns. Rural Montana homeowners evaluate state legislators on concrete delivery — water rights protection, rural road maintenance, school funding, and property tax management — not abstract positioning. A 72-28 margin is sustainable only if these concerns are being addressed; any challenger who articulates a clearer commitment to property owner economic interests is a genuine threat." },
          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access & Regional Medical Services", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district with 44.1-year median age and 82% homeownership in rural Montana faces real healthcare access challenges as aging residents require more sophisticated medical services that may not be available locally. Rural hospital viability, telemedicine infrastructure, and whether the state legislature is effectively partnering with federal healthcare systems to serve dispersed rural populations are concrete issues that affect this constituency's material quality of life. The college-educated 35.3% in this district understands healthcare policy complexity and will evaluate candidates on whether they have realistic solutions to rural medical access, not just rhetorical commitment." },
          { name: "Understanding the 28% Democratic Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "In a rural Montana Republican district, a 28% Democratic share is meaningful and suggests a specific constituency — whether young renters, college-educated professionals dissatisfied with current representation, or those frustrated with particular state legislative failures — that cannot be dismissed as noise. Any incumbent or challenger who does not understand specifically what is driving that 28% and why those voters reject the Republican position is missing the district's most important diagnostic signal. The margin is durable only if the Republican candidate is actively serving the reasons those 28% exist." },
          { name: "Treating a 72% Margin as a Stable Floor Without Investigating What Sustains It", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72-28 split in a rural Montana district can feel comfortable, but it is not explained by demographics alone — it represents active political choice by a college-educated, property-holding constituency that is evaluating performance, not following inherited party loyalty. Any candidate who assumes the 72% is safe because rural Montana is Republican, without understanding specifically what governance concerns the 28% represents and whether those concerns are being adequately addressed, is strategically vulnerable. A credible challenger who identifies those concerns and articulates solutions can shift this margin quickly." },
          { name: "Ignoring the College-Educated Constituency's Demand for Policy Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "With 35.3% college attainment — well above rural Montana average — this district includes professionals, educators, healthcare workers, and business operators who understand policy complexity and will not respond to simplistic messaging or vague positioning. Agricultural policy, water law, rural infrastructure investment, healthcare system design, and economic development strategy are the substantive issues this constituency cares about. Any candidate whose campaign relies on cultural signaling rather than detailed answers to these questions will struggle to maintain credibility with the college-educated plurality that enables a 72% Republican result." }
