          "The strategic vulnerability in a 55-45 result is that it leaves no room for erosion: a two-point swing in either direction determines control of the seat. The college-educated and renter populations are both electorally volatile — educated voters respond to specific policy performance and can shift if they perceive governance failure or misalignment on fiscal management, and renters are less bound by property-tax concerns that anchor homeowner Republicans, making them available to either party depending on economic anxiety and candidate credibility. A Republican challenger who can credibly argue that Ellis's governance has underdelivered on issues that matter to working professionals — housing affordability, workforce development, education quality — while simultaneously consolidating the 45% conservative base, has a plausible path to 52%. Conversely, Ellis's path to expanding beyond 55% requires deepening engagement with the college-educated constituency on specific policy results and with renters on housing costs and economic security. This is a seat where both candidates are one credible alternative away from losing.",
