          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Knowledge Economy", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 55.7% college attainment and $85,123 median household income, MT-SD-45 is a highly educated, professional constituency substantially above state and national averages. The University of Montana anchors Missoula's economy and culture, creating a voter base that evaluates candidates on policy substance, institutional competence, and alignment with professional-class economic interests. A 59% Republican result in a district this educated suggests the incumbent has successfully positioned on issues that resonate with college-educated voters—likely fiscal responsibility, property rights, or regulatory restraint—and the challenger must engage this constituency on its actual policy priorities rather than generic partisan messaging." },
          { name: "Renter Majority & Housing Cost Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 39.7% renters in a district with $85,123 median income, roughly two in five households lack the asset protection of homeownership and face direct exposure to housing cost inflation. In Missoula's tight rental market, renters at this income level are economically vulnerable to displacement and are evaluating candidates partly on whether they address housing affordability, rent stabilization, or landlord-tenant protections. The 59% Republican margin reflects the homeowner majority's preference, but the 41% opposition likely includes a substantial renter contingent whose material economic concerns—housing stability, affordability, access to services—differ measurably from homeowners'. Addressing renter economic security with specificity rather than dismissing it as a 'special interest' is both a governance obligation and a latent electoral opportunity." },
          { name: "Young, Mobile Professional Demographic & Demographic Flux", tag: "careful", why: "The 34.5-year median age combined with high college attainment and 39.7% renter rate signals a younger, mobile professional constituency—the exact demographic that drives population growth in mountain West college towns. This constituency is less bound by inherited partisan loyalty and evaluates candidates partly on whether the community (and policy environment) supports their economic mobility, professional development, and quality of life. If housing costs, local job quality, or service capacity become constraints on this demographic's ability to build stable adult lives in Missoula, their electoral behavior becomes less predictable; the 41% opposition may represent a concentrated preference among this group rather than peripheral dissent." },
          { name: "Treating College-Educated Voters as a Partisan Base Rather Than Substance-Driven Persuadables", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated voters—particularly in a university town at 55.7% attainment—are more likely to cross partisan lines on specific policy substance than less-educated cohorts. Assuming that the Republican 59% margin reflects stable partisan identification rather than agreement with particular positions on regulation, environmental policy, or fiscal governance invites a challenger to make inroads by offering a more credible or appealing policy alternative. The incumbent's 59% is not a mandate; it is a plurality of educated voters who prefer this candidate's approach to governance." },
          { name: "Dismissing Renter Concerns as a Partisan Urban Agenda", tag: "avoid", why: "At nearly 40% of the constituency, renters are not a fringe concern or a partisan wedge—they are a substantial economic segment whose housing stability directly affects their vote. Any messaging that frames renter concerns as illegitimate 'special interest' activism or positions housing policy as purely a market matter will alienate a material portion of the persuadable center and hand a challenger a clear opening to frame themselves as responsive to constituents' material needs. In a college town where young professionals comprise a significant renter share, this framing is strategically counterproductive." }
