          { name: "Housing Affordability & Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "The combination of 41-year median age, $67,258 income, and 37% renter rate signals a constituency under housing cost pressure even among college-educated professionals; this is not a wealthy district, but one where housing and property costs are consuming a significant share of household budgets. The 42% Republican share cannot be explained by education level alone and likely reflects frustration among both renters seeking affordable housing and homeowners facing property tax increases. Democrats in this district must offer affirmative housing policy that addresses both affordability for renters and tax fairness for homeowners, rather than defaulting to opposition to development or remaining silent on property tax structures that benefit neither group." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost Control", tag: "careful", why: "At 41-year median age with a college-educated professional majority, MT-SD-46 is likely to have significant Medicare and ACA enrollment, and healthcare cost control is a material concern for both younger professionals with family coverage needs and older professionals facing pre-Medicare costs. This is an issue where Democratic messaging has structural advantage — but only if the incumbent is offering concrete answers to healthcare cost inflation and provider access that go beyond defending current frameworks. The 58% result depends on college-educated professionals believing that Democrats are more competent on healthcare; if healthcare costs continue rising faster than incomes and Democratic messaging sounds defensive, the Republican opponent's cost-control positioning can erode the margin." },
          { name: "Partisan Identity Messaging Over Economic Materiality", tag: "avoid", why: "At $67,258 median income with 45.3% college attainment, this is a constituency that evaluates on economic security and professional-class concerns first, partisan identity second. Any Democratic campaign strategy that prioritizes cultural or identity-based messaging over concrete answers to housing affordability, property tax fairness, and healthcare cost control is ceding the terrain where the 42% Republican voters are most persuadable and where the 58% base is most vulnerable to erosion if Democrats sound out of touch with material constraints." },
          { name: "Assuming College Attainment Guarantees Democratic Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "While 45.3% college attainment is a strong predictor of Democratic support, 54.7% of the district does not have a college degree, and college-educated voters — particularly homeowners at 41 years old — are responsive to economic messaging about taxes, housing costs, and job quality if the incumbent is not addressing these concerns affirmatively. The 58% result is a real Democratic margin, but it is not guaranteed by demographics alone; it is conditional on the incumbent delivering concrete benefits to the constituency and on Republican messaging on cost-of-living issues remaining less salient than Democratic healthcare and education messaging. Treating college attainment as a floor rather than an active coalition to be consolidated and defended is the fastest path to losing what is currently a Democratic seat." }
