          { name: "College-Educated Swing Voter Coalition", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 39.5% college attainment and $76,755 median income, MT-SD-49 is an educated, upper-middle-income district where college-educated voters are the swing constituency. These voters prioritize policy substance, fiscal discipline, and demonstrated legislative competence over partisan identity — and they are increasingly persuadable by credible Republican candidates on taxes, regulation, and education quality. Curdy's 54% coalition includes educated voters who could shift to a credible alternative; defending this constituency requires specific legislative achievements on education funding, healthcare innovation, and fiscal responsibility articulated with policy detail, not partisan talking points." },
          { name: "Homeowner Tax & Property Value Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 71.7% homeownership and $76,755 income, the homeowner majority is evaluating whether government is delivering tangible value on K-12 education, infrastructure, and property tax fairness — the primary concerns of middle-class asset holders. A 54% coalition that includes homeowners is vulnerable to any challenger who credibly addresses property tax burden or education funding inequities; Curdy must articulate a specific record on these issues and preempt opposition framing on fiscal mismanagement or education underinvestment." },
          { name: "AIAN Community Engagement & Healthcare Access", tag: "careful", why: "The ~4.2% demographic gap most likely represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents whose electoral behavior and constituent needs are not fully captured in the 54% result. In Montana competitive districts, Native American voter participation and preference on tribal sovereignty, Indian Health Service access, and state-tribal economic development can shift outcomes by 3-4 points — arithmetically sufficient to change this race. Authentic engagement with tribal communities, not token acknowledgment, is foundational to holding MT-SD-49 in a genuinely competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Secure Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54% result is an eight-point margin in a district where 46% of voters actively chose the alternative — but in a constituency of educated swing voters, persuadable homeowners, and renters (28.3%), an 8-point margin is not a structural advantage but a current coalition vulnerable to challenger persuasion. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 54% represents a comfortable hold, rather than engaging the college-educated and homeowner opposition on specific policy grounds, risks allowing a credible challenger to consolidate the 46% into an organized base and peel swing voters away." },
          { name: "Partisan Default Over Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "MT-SD-49 is not a partisan stronghold where Democratic identity is sufficient to hold office; it is a professional, educated, upper-middle-income district where voters evaluate on governance competence and policy outcomes. A campaign that defaults to abstract Democratic messaging about social values or partisan opponents, rather than articulating specific legislative achievements on education, healthcare, and fiscal responsibility, will struggle to defend the college-educated and homeowner swing voters who determine elections in districts like this." }
